  "query_preview": "..A high-amplitude ridge has shifted east of Utah \ninto Colorado this evening while upstream, a couple of storm\nsystems are noted on water vapor satellite imagery. One is a\nclosed low off the SoCal coast, while a second, larger, closed \nlow is approaching the PacNW. The southern low will be coming \nonshore near the Cal/Baja coast by midday Friday. This system will\ngradually weaken as it becomes absorbed into the southerlies that\nwill strengthen ahead of the second storm, which will continue to\nmake its way onto the PacNW coast on Friday. The two storms will\neventually phase into a negatively tilted trough which will then\ncross Utah Friday night through Saturday.\n\nGiven the increased southerly flow, some moisture will spread\nnorth into Utah on Friday ahead of the southern stream storm. As \nsuch, expect isolated to scattered showers to develop Friday \nafternoon into Friday evening. This will take place under a\nrelatively mild airmass, with snow levels remaining mostly above \n8500 ft during this timeframe. Precipitation will then become more\nwidespread Friday night through Saturday morning ahead of and \nwith a relatively fast-moving cold front. Snow levels will \nquickly fall to around 6-7 kft by around midday Saturday. \nHowever, precipitation will mostly wind down by late morning or \nmidday Saturday behind the cold front, with the exception of the \nmountains, particularly those areas favored in northwesterly flow,\nwhich will maintain brief orographics into the afternoon. \nAdditionally, with warm temperatures over the GSL, expect some \nshowers to continue developing of the lake into the central \nWasatch Front and adjacent mountains as well.\n\nMost valleys are expected to see storm total precipitation \namounts between 0.4-0.7 inches near the I-15 corridor and less \nelsewhere. Meanwhile, mountains are expected to see 0.6-1.2 inches\nwith locally higher amounts in the upper Cottonwoods and near \nBrian Head. Snow amounts remain generally in the 4-8 inch range, \nmainly above 7500 feet, with higher amounts possible in the upper \nCottonwoods, Tushars, and near Brian Head. \n\nA transient ridge will cross the area late Saturday through\nMonday, bringing a break in the weather. However, the pattern\nremains progressive, with the next storm system looking to impact\nthe area during the Monday night through Tuesday night timeframe.\nThis will be another relatively fast-moving system which will also\nbring widespread precipitation to the area. However, it looks like\nthis could be a colder system, which will result in lower snow \nlevels, potentially down to at least 5000 feet. \n\nAfter a brief break Wednesday into Wednesday night, yet another \nstorm system could impact the area by Thursday morning. There is\ngood agreement in the large-scale pattern regarding the existence\nof this late week storm, but with uncertainties in the timing and\nevolution. Latest global deterministics are leaning towards this \nstorm being a broad closed low which will slowly move through the \nregion through the weekend, bringing an extended period of \nprecipitation to the forecast area.",
